"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. The preoperative PSA was elevated at 8.7. The surgical findings included a Gleason 4+3 with tertiary pattern 5 (ISUP grade group 3), no extraprostatic extension, positive surgical margins, and no seminal vesicle invasion. Absence of lymph nodes removed is noted, which widens uncertainty as nodal sampling was not performed. Biopsy results showed a difference between the initial Gleason 0+0 and the later Gleason 3+4 with perineural invasion.\n\nCountervailing factors include the absence of lymphovascular invasion and the absence of extraprostatic extension. The positive surgical margins represent a risk factor. The postoperative PSA trajectory and subsequent follow-up are unknown, which is a significant factor limiting prognosis assessment. Missing nodal sampling further complicates the certainty regarding local disease control. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent trajectory would be crucial in revising this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 85.78 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 55.76–115.81 months; its coverage has not been validated."
